---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { getPath } from '../../utils/paths';

const snippet = `curl -fsSL https://krabka.io/quickstart/docker-compose.yml -o docker-compose.yml && docker compose up -d`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Introduction"
  description="Krabka is an open-source, byte-exact Apache Kafka wire-protocol broker, native KRaft consensus engine, and polyglot streaming platform written in Rust."
  currentSlug="/docs"
>
  <div class="space-y-6 font-sans">
    <div class="border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-white sm:text-4xl">Krabka Documentation</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-400 mt-2 text-sm max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        The complete technical guide to building, operating, and streaming with Krabka.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-6 text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white">Krabka</strong> is a clean-sheet, memory-safe implementation of the Apache Kafka streaming platform. Designed to eliminate the operational overhead, memory pressure, and garbage collection pauses of the JVM, Krabka delivers byte-exact wire-protocol parity across all Kafka APIs while operating in a fraction of the memory.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Key Architectural Pillars</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Byte-exact wire parity:</strong> Codecs generated directly from upstream Apache Kafka JSON schemas. Compatible with standard <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">kafka-clients</code>, JVM CLI tools, and existing producer/consumer applications.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Native KRaft quorum:</strong> Metadata is replicated via a native Raft implementation (<code class="font-mono text-krab-300">krabka-raft</code>) implementing the KIP-595 RPC protocol with zero ZooKeeper dependency.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Tiered storage subsystem:</strong> Asynchronously offloads sealed partition segments to Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, or MinIO with local NVMe caching.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Polyglot streams & Arrow memory:</strong> Native stream processing libraries for Go, Java, and Rust featuring zero-copy Apache Arrow batch processing and barrier cuts.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Empirical Validation</h2>
      <p>
        See our <a href={getPath('/docs/benchmarks')} class="text-krab-400 hover:text-krab-300 font-semibold underline">Benchmark Methodology & Performance</a> guide for reproducible comparisons against JVM Kafka across memory RSS, KRaft failover, and saturated throughput.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Architectural Deep Dives & Whitepapers</h2>
      <p>
        For engineering rationale, scaling envelopes, and formal substrate guarantees:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
        <li>
          <a href={getPath('/whitepapers/gres-scaling')} class="text-krab-400 hover:text-krab-300 font-semibold underline">Scaling Postgres on a Kafka Substrate:</a> How Krabka Gres scales Postgres-compatible writes across tenants, ranges, and single tables with no per-tenant consensus and no synchronized clocks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getPath('/docs/runtime-architecture')} class="text-krab-400 hover:text-krab-300 font-semibold underline">Runtime Architecture:</a> Operational topology, control plane reconcile loops, and data plane interactions between broker nodes, schema registry, and operator.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Continuous Documentation Pipeline</h2>
      <p>
        The Krabka documentation hub is decoupled from individual sub-repositories and synchronized automatically:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Decentralized authoring:</strong> Architecture and engineering guides are maintained directly alongside the code in each component repository (<code class="font-mono text-krab-300">krabka-broker</code>, <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">krabka-streams-java</code>, and <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">krabka-streams-go</code>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Native compiler reference trees:</strong> Multi-crate Rustdoc, multi-module Javadoc, and static Godoc trees are generated hermetically by language toolchains and hosted under <a href={getPath('/api')} class="text-krab-400 hover:text-krab-300 font-semibold underline">/api</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Nightly & release publishing:</strong> The website is built nightly at 04:00 UTC and triggered automatically on new GitHub releases, ingesting newly merged guide commits and updated compiler archives without manual intervention.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Quick Run</h2>
      <div class="frosted-card rounded-xl p-5 border border-white/10 space-y-3">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between text-xs text-gray-400">
          <span>Start local single-node broker:</span>
          <button type="button" class="copy-btn rounded bg-white/5 px-2 py-1 text-gray-300 hover:bg-white/10 hover:text-white transition-colors text-xs" data-copy={snippet}>
            Copy
          </button>
        </div>
        <pre class="bg-black/50 p-4 rounded-lg font-mono text-xs text-gray-200 overflow-x-auto border border-white/5"><code>$ {snippet}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Next Steps</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose pt-1">
        <a href={getPath('/docs/quickstart')} class="p-4 rounded-xl frosted-card border border-white/10 hover:border-krab-500/50 transition-colors block">
          <div class="text-sm font-bold text-white">Quickstart Guide →</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1">Spin up a local broker cluster with Docker Compose or deploy to Kubernetes via Helm.</p>
        </a>
        <a href={getPath('/docs/architecture')} class="p-4 rounded-xl frosted-card border border-white/10 hover:border-krab-500/50 transition-colors block">
          <div class="text-sm font-bold text-white">Ecosystem Architecture →</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1">Explore repository dependency layering, crate boundaries, and wire-protocol modularity.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
